**Theorem 5.6 (exact general selection and name completeness).** At an
authentic cover, AcquisitionOffset succeeds exactly for
$0\le t\le b-a$, binding the unique $k=a+t$. For every authentic completed
auxiliary occurrence $m$, SourceOffset succeeds exactly for
$0\le t\le f-e$, binding the unique $k=e+t$. Thus every cut in $[a,b]$
has a finite AcquisitionOffset name, and every cut of the completed
source fragment has a finite source-relative name. Evaluation requires
that exact finite name to be actually supplied.

Proof. Paired unary cancellation returns the ordinary nonnegative
differences, and Cat on the internal naturals returns their sums, by
their explicit loop invariants. Hence the first range test is equivalent
to $a\le a+t\le b$; translation is a bijection on the integer intervals.
Theorem 5.5 gives the same fact for the internally constructed $e,f$.
For any specified $k\in[a,b]$, choose the finite $t=k-a$; for a specified
source-fragment cut choose $t=k-e$ and its actual acquired $m$.
These choices prove name existence and uniqueness of each name's result.
They do not give an owner those target words without supplying, retaining
or computing them. Definition 3.1 supplies the actual later acquisition
of the name, while source resolution and bounded replay compute its
absolute binding internally. In particular an identical archive with
two different possible intended offsets does not choose between them;
CAP Proposition 13.5's intention distinction remains. ∎

